而且他无法逃脱、反抗或做任何类似的事情，而且他是一个如此令人印象深刻的人。他还是个神职人员。一个神职人员等等。这解释了为什么他会成为如此大的丑闻。几个月后，希尔实际上离开了去利比里亚。他和其他大约100名非裔美国人一起去了，其中许多人来自约克县。所以那是在非洲。

97
00:49:46.000 --> 00:50:10.000
Yeah. So Liberia and West Africa. and he told a congressional committee before he went. He said, we're going because, quote, we do not believe it is possible for our people to live in this country peaceably. And his story ended very sadly because, like a lot of people who emigrated to Liberia at the time, he got malaria pretty much straight away and he died after six months. And that was the end of him.
是的。所以利比里亚在西非。他在离开前告诉国会委员会。他说，我们离开是因为——引用他的话——我们不相信我们的人民有可能在这个国家和平生活。他的故事结局非常悲惨，因为像当时许多移民到利比里亚的人一样，他几乎立刻就得了疟疾，六个月后就去世了。这就是他的结局。

98
00:50:10.000 --> 00:50:47.000
And for Major Merrill, the Tom Holland of this story, the attack on Hill is pretty much the last straw. He summoned a group of the sort of bigwigs of U.S. York County, and he said, I know some of you are Klansman. If you do not stop this now, Washington will take really drastic measures. But of course, the question is, will Washington ever take the measures that are necessary? So Ulysses S. Grant has been president since March 1869. He's been walking this very fine line, because on the one hand, he's the hero of the north, he's the champion of radical reconstruction, he has delivered the 15th Amendment, which
对于梅里尔少校，这个故事里的汤姆·霍兰德来说，对希尔的袭击几乎是压死骆驼的最后一根稻草。他召集了一群约克县的大人物，他说，我知道你们中有些人是三K党。如果你们现在不停止，华盛顿将采取非常严厉的措施。但当然，问题是，华盛顿会采取必要的措施吗？所以尤利西斯·S·格兰特自1869年3月以来一直担任总统。他一直走在一条非常微妙的线上，因为一方面，他是北方的英雄，是激进重建的捍卫者，他通过了第15修正案，该修正案

99
00:50:47.000 --> 00:51:12.000
supposedly gives black men the right to vote. On the other hand, Grant's campaign slogan was, let us have peace. And that implies that this whole business is going to go away. His cabinet is full of relative moderates. Personally, he's pragmatic and he's quite cautious and conservative and so on.
